{"module":"conquest","voice":"male","duration":251.2,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Discovery and conquest","start":0.0,"end":1.9,"words":[[0.05,0.8],[0.8,1.04],[1.04,1.71]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Before there was a color line in North America, there was a land line.","start":2.7,"end":8.21,"words":[[2.75,3.12],[3.12,3.39],[3.39,3.66],[3.66,3.84],[3.84,4.21],[4.21,4.57],[4.57,4.75],[4.75,5.02],[5.02,5.75],[6.07,6.34],[6.34,6.61],[6.61,6.79],[6.79,7.15],[7.15,7.61]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The idea that Christian Europeans could claim lands already lived on became U.S. law, and every Native nation lived with the consequences.","start":8.56,"end":17.59,"words":[[8.61,8.75],[8.75,9.12],[9.12,9.34],[9.34,9.93],[9.93,10.67],[10.67,10.89],[10.89,11.26],[11.26,11.63],[11.63,12.07],[12.07,12.37],[12.37,12.52],[12.52,12.96],[12.96,13.33],[13.7,13.92],[14.17,14.4],[14.4,14.69],[14.69,15.13],[15.13,15.58],[15.58,15.87],[15.87,16.09],[16.09,16.24],[16.24,17.2]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Nations, not wilderness","start":17.94,"end":20.83,"words":[[17.99,18.93],[19.34,19.69],[19.69,20.62]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Hundreds of nations with their own governments, trade networks and cities lived here.","start":21.53,"end":27.31,"words":[[21.58,22.19],[22.19,22.34],[22.34,22.88],[22.88,23.11],[23.11,23.34],[23.34,23.56],[23.56,24.4],[24.67,25.05],[25.05,25.59],[25.59,25.81],[25.81,26.2],[26.2,26.5],[26.5,26.81]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Cahokia, near present-day St. Louis, had perhaps 10,000 to 20,000 people around 1100 CE.","start":27.66,"end":34.23,"words":[[27.68,28.21],[28.42,28.6],[28.6,29.19],[29.19,29.48],[29.78,30.01],[30.22,30.39],[30.39,30.75],[30.75,31.34],[31.34,31.46],[31.46,32.22],[32.22,32.52],[32.52,32.87],[32.87,33.64],[33.64,33.81]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Disease, war and enslavement followed contact; by 1900 the U.S. census counted only about 237,000 Native people.","start":34.58,"end":42.99,"words":[[34.63,34.99],[35.19,35.31],[35.31,35.48],[35.48,36.13],[36.13,36.48],[36.48,36.95],[37.16,37.33],[37.33,38.15],[38.15,38.27],[38.27,38.56],[38.86,39.21],[39.21,39.62],[39.62,39.91],[39.91,40.2],[40.2,41.79],[41.79,42.14],[42.14,42.49]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Historian Andrés Reséndez estimates that 2.5 to 5 million Native people were enslaved across the Americas between 1492 and 1900, a figure that is still debated.","start":43.34,"end":56.74,"words":[[43.38,44.1],[44.1,44.66],[44.66,45.38],[45.38,46.03],[46.03,46.27],[46.27,47.15],[47.15,47.31],[47.31,47.63],[47.63,48.11],[48.11,48.59],[48.59,48.99],[48.99,49.15],[49.15,49.79],[49.79,50.19],[50.19,50.36],[50.36,51.0],[51.0,51.48],[51.48,52.52],[52.52,52.76],[52.76,53.96],[54.24,54.4],[54.4,54.72],[54.72,54.97],[54.97,55.13],[55.13,55.45],[55.45,56.17]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Discovery becomes law; removal follows","start":57.64,"end":61.05,"words":[[57.69,58.51],[58.51,59.07],[59.07,59.34],[59.66,60.31],[60.31,60.86]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Johnson v. M'Intosh (1823) the Supreme Court held that “discovery” gave the U.S. title to Native land.","start":61.75,"end":69.34,"words":[[61.8,61.94],[61.94,62.41],[62.41,62.61],[62.95,63.42],[63.42,64.37],[64.61,64.74],[64.74,65.15],[65.15,65.35],[65.35,65.62],[65.62,65.82],[65.82,66.43],[66.43,66.7],[66.7,66.84],[66.84,67.17],[67.51,67.92],[67.92,68.05],[68.05,68.46],[68.46,68.8]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Worcester v. Georgia (1832) it ruled that Georgia had no authority over Cherokee land, but President Jackson's government enforced the Indian Removal Act (1830) anyway.","start":69.69,"end":82.0,"words":[[69.74,69.89],[69.89,70.27],[70.27,70.49],[70.87,71.31],[71.31,72.14],[72.4,72.55],[72.55,72.85],[72.85,73.07],[73.07,73.52],[73.52,73.75],[73.75,73.97],[73.97,74.49],[74.49,74.79],[74.79,75.39],[75.39,75.77],[76.03,76.25],[76.25,76.93],[76.93,77.52],[77.52,78.2],[78.2,78.65],[78.65,78.8],[78.8,79.24],[79.24,79.77],[79.77,79.99],[79.99,80.67],[80.93,81.45]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Cherokee, Chickasaw, Choctaw, Muscogee (Creek) and Seminole nations were forced west; thousands of Cherokee died on the Trail of Tears in 1838–39.","start":82.35,"end":91.97,"words":[[82.4,82.52],[82.52,83.08],[83.3,83.8],[84.02,84.45],[84.67,85.23],[85.23,85.48],[85.69,85.88],[85.88,86.38],[86.38,86.81],[86.81,86.94],[86.94,87.18],[87.18,87.5],[87.71,88.21],[88.21,88.33],[88.33,88.83],[88.83,89.08],[89.08,89.2],[89.2,89.33],[89.33,89.64],[89.64,89.76],[89.76,90.01],[90.01,90.14],[90.14,91.63]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Some of these nations had adopted slavery, and enslaved Black people were forced along the same trails.","start":92.32,"end":99.43,"words":[[92.36,92.61],[92.61,92.77],[92.77,93.01],[93.01,93.57],[93.57,93.81],[93.81,94.37],[94.37,95.1],[95.38,95.62],[95.62,96.26],[96.26,96.59],[96.59,96.99],[96.99,97.15],[97.15,97.47],[97.47,97.79],[97.79,97.95],[97.95,98.27],[98.27,98.84]]},{"el":"h2","text":"War and extermination","start":100.33,"end":102.48,"words":[[100.38,100.6],[100.6,100.93],[100.93,102.35]]},{"el":"s2","text":"At Sand Creek, Colorado, in 1864, U.S. volunteers killed more than 150 Cheyenne and Arapaho people, most of them women, children and elders, at a camp flying a U.S. flag.","start":103.18,"end":118.41,"words":[[103.23,103.38],[103.38,103.69],[103.69,104.07],[104.34,105.11],[105.38,105.53],[105.53,106.61],[106.88,107.26],[107.65,108.34],[108.34,108.65],[108.65,108.8],[108.8,109.03],[109.03,110.34],[110.34,110.8],[110.8,111.03],[111.03,111.64],[111.64,112.1],[112.37,112.76],[112.76,112.91],[112.91,113.14],[113.14,113.6],[113.87,114.49],[114.49,114.72],[114.72,115.18],[115.45,115.6],[115.6,115.75],[115.75,116.06],[116.06,116.52],[116.52,116.68],[116.68,117.06],[117.45,117.83]]},{"el":"s2","text":"At Wounded Knee in 1890, soldiers killed about 250 or more Lakota.","start":118.76,"end":124.53,"words":[[118.81,118.96],[118.96,119.4],[119.4,119.54],[119.54,119.69],[119.69,120.57],[120.83,121.42],[121.42,121.71],[121.71,122.08],[122.08,123.25],[123.25,123.33],[123.33,123.47],[123.47,124.06]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In California, Governor Peter Burnett declared in 1851 that “a war of extermination will continue to be waged”; state-funded militias killed thousands, and the Native population fell from about 150,000 to about 30,000 between 1846 and 1870.","start":124.88,"end":140.46,"words":[[124.93,125.05],[125.05,125.67],[125.88,126.25],[126.25,126.5],[126.5,126.8],[126.8,127.23],[127.23,127.35],[127.35,128.15],[128.15,128.34],[128.34,128.46],[128.46,128.58],[128.58,128.7],[128.7,129.5],[129.5,129.69],[129.69,130.18],[130.18,130.3],[130.3,130.42],[130.42,130.85],[131.07,131.74],[131.74,132.17],[132.17,132.42],[132.42,132.97],[133.19,133.37],[133.37,133.49],[133.49,133.86],[133.86,134.54],[134.54,134.72],[134.72,134.97],[134.97,135.27],[135.27,136.75],[136.75,136.87],[136.87,137.17],[137.17,137.85],[137.85,138.22],[138.22,139.02],[139.02,139.2],[139.2,140.0]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2019 California's governor apologized and called it genocide.","start":140.81,"end":146.09,"words":[[140.85,141.01],[141.01,142.03],[142.03,142.81],[142.81,143.28],[143.28,144.14],[144.14,144.37],[144.37,144.69],[144.69,144.84],[144.84,145.63]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Allotment and termination","start":146.99,"end":149.15,"words":[[147.04,147.79],[147.79,148.07],[148.07,149.01]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Dawes Act (1887) broke reservations into individual plots and sold the “surplus.”","start":149.85,"end":156.18,"words":[[149.89,150.04],[150.04,150.26],[150.26,150.47],[150.47,151.49],[151.75,152.11],[152.11,152.91],[152.91,153.2],[153.2,154.0],[154.0,154.36],[154.36,154.58],[154.58,154.94],[154.94,155.09],[155.09,155.6]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Native-held land fell from about 138 million acres in 1887 to about 48 million by 1934.","start":156.53,"end":163.55,"words":[[156.57,157.16],[157.16,157.39],[157.39,157.57],[157.57,157.8],[157.8,158.1],[158.1,159.27],[159.27,159.62],[159.62,159.91],[159.91,160.03],[160.03,160.85],[160.85,160.97],[160.97,161.26],[161.26,161.73],[161.73,162.08],[162.08,162.26],[162.26,163.08]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In the 1950s, “termination” policy ended federal recognition of more than 100 tribes, and the Relocation program moved Native families to cities with promises of jobs that often didn't exist.","start":163.9,"end":177.3,"words":[[163.95,164.1],[164.1,164.25],[164.25,165.3],[165.56,166.31],[166.31,166.76],[166.76,167.13],[167.13,167.65],[167.65,168.4],[168.4,168.55],[168.55,168.7],[168.7,168.92],[168.92,169.67],[169.67,170.19],[170.45,170.68],[170.68,170.83],[170.83,171.65],[171.65,172.24],[172.24,172.54],[172.54,172.99],[172.99,173.51],[173.51,173.66],[173.66,174.04],[174.04,174.26],[174.26,174.86],[174.86,175.01],[175.01,175.38],[175.38,175.61],[175.61,175.9],[175.9,176.28],[176.28,176.8]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Boarding schools","start":178.2,"end":179.63,"words":[[178.25,178.83],[178.83,179.41]]},{"el":"s4","text":"“Kill the Indian in him, and save the man,” said Richard Henry Pratt, who founded the Carlisle Indian Industrial School in 1879.","start":180.33,"end":189.97,"words":[[180.36,180.6],[180.6,180.76],[180.76,181.23],[181.23,181.39],[181.39,181.71],[181.98,182.22],[182.22,182.54],[182.54,182.7],[182.7,183.01],[183.01,183.25],[183.25,183.72],[183.72,184.12],[184.12,184.52],[184.79,184.95],[184.95,185.5],[185.5,185.66],[185.66,186.22],[186.22,186.69],[186.69,187.56],[187.56,187.88],[187.88,188.04],[188.04,189.38]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The federal government ran or supported 408 boarding schools that took children from their families, banned their languages and religions, and used harsh discipline.","start":190.32,"end":200.58,"words":[[190.36,190.5],[190.5,190.99],[190.99,191.62],[191.62,191.83],[191.83,191.91],[191.91,192.4],[192.4,193.38],[193.38,193.74],[193.74,194.09],[194.09,194.3],[194.3,194.51],[194.51,195.07],[195.07,195.35],[195.35,195.57],[195.57,196.13],[196.38,196.66],[196.66,196.87],[196.87,197.57],[197.57,197.78],[197.78,198.49],[198.73,198.95],[198.95,199.23],[199.23,199.44],[199.44,200.07]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The Interior Department's investigation documented at least 973 deaths of children (2024).","start":200.93,"end":207.54,"words":[[200.97,201.09],[201.09,201.62],[201.62,202.22],[202.22,202.99],[202.99,203.64],[203.64,203.76],[203.76,204.0],[204.0,205.37],[205.37,205.6],[205.6,205.72],[205.72,206.2],[206.2,207.09]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In October 2024 President Biden formally apologized.","start":207.89,"end":212.41,"words":[[207.94,208.08],[208.08,208.58],[208.58,209.57],[209.57,210.21],[210.21,210.64],[210.64,211.07],[211.07,211.92]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Resistance and sovereignty today","start":213.31,"end":215.61,"words":[[213.36,214.11],[214.11,214.35],[214.35,215.1],[215.1,215.51]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Native activists occupied Alcatraz (1969–71) and Wounded Knee (1973), and won the Indian Self-Determination Act (1975).","start":216.31,"end":227.64,"words":[[216.36,216.8],[216.8,217.46],[217.46,218.04],[218.04,218.62],[218.62,220.74],[221.0,221.22],[221.22,221.65],[221.65,221.8],[221.8,223.11],[223.37,223.59],[223.59,223.81],[223.81,223.95],[223.95,224.39],[224.39,225.56],[225.56,225.78],[225.78,227.16]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In McGirt v. Oklahoma (2020) the Supreme Court held that much of eastern Oklahoma remains Muscogee (Creek) reservation for purposes of federal criminal law.","start":227.99,"end":239.33,"words":[[228.04,228.19],[228.19,228.57],[228.57,228.8],[229.18,229.93],[229.93,230.84],[231.11,231.26],[231.26,231.71],[231.71,231.94],[231.94,232.24],[232.24,232.47],[232.47,232.77],[232.77,232.92],[232.92,233.3],[233.3,234.05],[234.05,234.58],[234.58,235.26],[235.26,235.57],[235.83,236.59],[236.59,236.74],[236.74,237.27],[237.27,237.42],[237.42,237.95],[237.95,238.55],[238.55,238.78]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The protests at Standing Rock (2016) drew thousands of supporters, and in 2021 Deb Haaland (Laguna Pueblo) became the first Native American cabinet secretary.","start":239.68,"end":250.26,"words":[[239.73,239.86],[239.86,240.45],[240.45,240.58],[240.58,241.05],[241.05,241.25],[241.25,242.11],[242.34,242.53],[242.53,243.06],[243.06,243.2],[243.2,243.72],[243.96,244.15],[244.15,244.29],[244.29,245.28],[245.28,245.48],[245.48,245.87],[245.87,246.27],[246.27,246.73],[246.96,247.36],[247.36,247.49],[247.49,247.76],[247.76,248.15],[248.15,248.68],[248.68,249.15],[249.15,249.74]]}]}